9037: ## Round 485 (#485) -- 242 of 256 opcodes ended a command and never raised its interrupt
9038: 
9039: > ### *** THIS ROUND SHIPPED WITHOUT A PASS 2, AND A GATE CAUGHT IT RATHER THAN I DID. ***
9040: >
9041: > It went from pass-1 design straight to implementation and commit. `precommit_check.sh`
9042: > refused the **next** commit until a pass-2 receipt for `d819188` existed.
9043: >
9044: > **The pass then paid for the rule in one use.** It found three things, from three different
9045: > seats, one of them a substitute transport that cannot read a file:
9046: >
9047: > * **`M10_seclock`** -- the SEC arm's assert behind a latch of its own -- **escaped all four
9048: >   legs**, 128 rows, 0 failures. Fixed in-round with a fifth leg.
9049: > * an **L4 in the shipped comment**: a boot census resting on an artifact that was not
9050: >   preserved.
9051: > * a **second L4 in that same sentence**, which my correction quoted verbatim and so carried
9052: >   forward: **`0xE0` is `WDCC_STANDBY_IMMED` and has no case**, so it lands in `default:`,
9053: >   not in the SEC arm where the comment said *here*. One arm's comment claimed the other
9054: >   arm's traffic.
9055: >
9056: > *Recorded here and not only in the round's table, because the CHANGELOG is what a reader
9057: > consults, and without this the block reads as a normal lifecycle.*
9058: 
9059: `wdc_command()` has five arms that terminate a command with `WDCE_ABRT`. Two never raised the
9060: completion interrupt: the six `WDCC_SEC_*` opcodes and `default:`. Two statements, two arms.
9061: Detector **128 rows**, gate **PASS (334 checks)**, kill table **7 of 7**.
9062: 
9063: ### The warrant is an invariant, not an analogy
9064: 
9065: Derived over all 256 opcodes rather than from a few neighbours:
9066: 
9067: > **Every command `wdc_command()` finishes raises the completion interrupt.** The only opcodes
9068: > that stay silent are the three that hand control back for a data phase �?" `WDCC_WRITE`,
9069: > `WDCC_WRITEMULTI`, `ATAPI_PKT_CMD` �?" and each asserts when its data phase drains.
9070: 
9071: The 242 were the only violations. And the two defect arms were **the only terminal arms in the
9072: function with no interrupt decision recorded at all** �?" no assert, no comment. Six arms carry a
9073: `TODO �?� interrupt` hedge; in five the author asserted, and in the sixth he left the assert
9074: *commented out*, so that decision is visible in the source. A question never reached, not a
9075: decision to stay silent.
9076: 
9077: No specification is cited and none is available. A sibling model settles the direction:
9078: `dev_mb89352.c` asserts on any non-zero interrupt status regardless of success.
9079: 
9080: ### My first detector was below the floor, and it took a seat to measure it
9081: 
9082: I shipped a two-observable sweep �?" ABRT and assert-count, 256 opcodes. **Four of seven mutants
9083: read `ABRT+NO-irq = 0` and pass it entirely:**
9084: 
9085: | mutant | killed by |
9086: |---|---|
9087: | `M5_inlatch` �?" assert moved inside the unimplemented-command latch | leg 2, a **second command on one device** |
9088: | `M4_hoist` �?" one assert after the switch | the **identity pin** on the silent set |
9089: | `M7_noclear` �?" STATUS read stops clearing the request | leg 3, **retraction** |
9090: | `M8_sfabortonly` �?" SET_FEATURES asserts only on abort | leg 4, **`precomp = WDSF_SET_MODE`** |
9091: 
9092: `M4_hoist` is the sharp one: it "fixes" all 242 and starts asserting **at dispatch** for the
9093: three deferred opcodes �?" a spurious completion interrupt on every disk write a real guest
9094: issues. A count-only oracle passes it, so the silent set is pinned as an **identity**
9095: `{0x30, 0xa0, 0xc5}`, not a count.
9096: 
9097: `M5` cannot be seen by a sweep at *any* oracle width: one fresh device per opcode means the
9098: latch is 0 on every leg. `M8` is the input-coverage lesson �?" the sweep varies the command byte
9099: and never `precomp`, so SET_FEATURES' success path never ran. **Input coverage in one dimension
9100: is not input coverage.**
9101: 
9102: And `M9_notailtick` is the one my *reproduction* could not see: it called `wdc_command()` and
9103: ran the tick by hand, so a fix whose repair depends on `dev_wdc_access()`'s tail tick was
9104: invisible. Leg 1 now drives the real register path �?" a command store.
9105: 
9106: ### Severity, corrected downward against my own earlier record
9107: 
9108: `WDCS_BSY` is **never set anywhere in `dev_wdc.c`**, so a polling driver's wait returns at once
9109: with `DRDY|DSC|ERR`. OpenBSD 7.6 reaches both arms routinely �?" `0xF5` on every attach, `0xE0` on
9110: every halt �?" and completes boot and shutdown against the old behaviour. **There is no measured
9111: hang.** Nine ledger entries carrying "waits forever" were corrected in place. The licence is
9112: protocol correctness at trivial cost.
9113: 
9114: ### Witness
9115: 
9116: A seat built and measured a **rung-3** witness on unmodified `-E landisk`: 2/5 pre-fix, 5/5
9117: post-fix, with liveness, attribution and device-signature controls. **Its script was not
