11622: ## Round 465 (#465) -- dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c: `data` and `data_len` were released apart
11623: 
11624: `alloc_data()` sets the two together -- `d->data_len = d->cnt;` then the allocation. **Both
11625: release sites cleared only the POINTER**, leaving `data_len` describing a buffer that no longer
11626: exists. Every later reader tests one field and indexes with the other, so a stale length is a
11627: read of freed or absent memory, and a guest PIO sequence reached it: nine accesses on the cheap
11628: route, eighteen on the staged route, both ending in SIGSEGV of the host process.
11629: 
11630: **THE FIX IS OPTION B, THE `free_data()` HELPER -- and the ADDITION LICENCE governs it, not
11631: minimal-surgical.** A helper is an addition to product code, so it needs a measured delta or a
11632: transferred warrant. It has both. *** THE LICENCE'S MEASURED DELTA IS STRONGER THAN THIS BLOCK FIRST CITED, and the
11633: evidence was already committed. *** The delta is not a 16/16 sweep -- the sibling
11634: mutant reproduces that identically, so it measures nothing about A-vs-B. It is
11635: `E:mism how` in `killtable_20260826/`: the SAME one-word edit gives **sig11 on
11636: `m_ret415`** (the defect measurably returns under the rejected tail form) and **exit0
11637: on `m_helper_ret`** (it does not under the shipped helper). Same edit, both candidate
11638: fixes, opposite outcomes. Found by the pass-2 Fable seat in this round's own logs.
11273: ## Round 473 (#473) -- the ATAPI DATAIN chunking pushed a whole transfer into a ring that cannot hold it, and then reported zero bytes left
11274: 
11275: Two defects in one piece of machinery, closed by one diff.  The adjudication took them as ONE
11276: round with TWO row ids: the arm and the re-arm are the two halves of a single chunking protocol,
11277: and bounding the push WITHOUT repairing the re-arm would have injected new corruption into the
11278: (32K, 64K] band that works today.
11279: 
11280:   `wdcringambig`  the initial arm pushed every byte of the transfer into a 513-block ring
11281:   `wdcrearmzero`  the re-arm reported ZERO bytes remaining and the command never completed
11282: 
11283: ### What was wrong
11284: 
11285: `WDC_INBUF_SIZE` is `512 * (WDC_MAX_SECTORS + 1)` -- exactly 513 blocks, 262656 bytes.  The
11286: initial arm did `atapi_len = data_in_len`, pushed every byte, and clamped to 32768 **afterwards**.
11287: The clamp was not missing; it was on the wrong side of the loop.
11288: 
11289: At the re-arm, `if (atapi_len > 32768) atapi_len = 0;` -- where the initial arm clamps to 32768
11178: ## Round 474 (#474) -- a guest READ of the footbridge timer-clear register performed the clear
11179: 
11180: `case TIMER_1_CLEAR:` in `dev_footbridge.c` carried **no `writeflag` test at all**, while
11181: `case TIMER_1_CONTROL:` immediately above it does. So a guest READ ran the whole body: it
11182: reloaded the counter when periodic, decremented the pending-interrupt count, and DEASSERTED the
11183: interrupt. A guest that merely READ the register silently acknowledged a pending timer interrupt.
11184: 
11185: **The guest-visible consequence is a LOST interrupt, not a bad value** -- which is why it survived:
11186: nothing returns wrong data, a timer just stops firing.
11187: 
11188: FOUR addresses, not one: the timer fold normalises 0x30C / 0x32C / 0x34C / 0x36C onto this case.
11189: 
11190: ### Confirmed against the datasheet, not inferred from the code
11191: 
11192: DC21285 section 7.3.42, verbatim:
11193: 
11194:     Any write to the TimerNClear register (any data) clears the associated interrupt.
10908: ## Round 475 (#475) -- the ATAPI byte counter stepped past zero, and the transfer length is a guest-chosen byte
10909: 
10910: `atapi_len -= consumed` had no bound against what REMAINS. With an odd remainder a four-byte
10911: data-port read drives the counter negative, so `if (atapi_len == 0)` never fires again and the
10912: command never completes -- while `atapi_received` ends up larger than `data_in_len`, so the
10913: device claims it delivered more bytes than the transfer holds.
10914: 
10915: **MEASURED on the committed tree, before any edit:**
10916: 
10917:     FAIL odd chunk: the count stops at zero      atapi_len = -1, want 0
10918:     FAIL odd chunk: received never exceeds len   received = 4, want 3
10919: 
10920: ### This is `#341`'s class surviving at `#341`'s own site
10921: 
10922: `#341` fixed `consumed` to count what the reads actually POP (`len == 4 ? 4 : (len >= 2 ? 2 : 1)`)
10923: rather than what the guest asked for -- and **its own comment describes exactly this symptom** as
10924: the thing being fixed: *"let atapi_len step PAST zero, which its `== 0` test then never catches."*
10747: ## Round 476 (#476) -- #475's clamp WOKE a NULL dereference, and two guest accesses killed the host
10748: 
10749: **A regression I shipped one round earlier.** `#475` clamped `consumed` so the ATAPI byte
10750: counter could no longer step past zero. That was right -- and it made `atapi_len` STAY at 0
10751: where it had previously gone negative, so `if (d->atapi_len == 0)` began firing on a path the
10752: negative counter had kept dead. That path dereferences `atapi_st` without checking it.
10753: 
10754: MEASURED: SIGSEGV at `0x48`, which is `offsetof(struct scsi_transfer, data_in_len)`. Two
10755: guest data-port accesses with no ATAPI transfer armed. The bisect is symmetric -- the kill
10756: appears exactly at `#475` and is absent before it.
10757: 
10758: THE FIX: decline and latch, per this tree's own idiom -- a device that cannot service an
10759: access must not take the emulator with it (`#337`, `#184`, `#186`, `#315`, `#316`, `#326`).
10760: The guard reads `atapi_len == 0 && atapi_st == NULL`, and the message carries `(once)`, which
10761: the 2026-08-26 census established as the tree's plurality suppression marker.
10762: 
10763: **DID IT CLOSE THE CLASS OR ONE INSTANCE?** `#475` taught that repairing a counter can wake
10569: ## Round 477 (#477) -- a guest data-port WRITE could free the ATAPI transfer the device was still delivering
10570: 
10571: `atapi_cmd_in_progress` is set when a packet command arrives and cleared only when the NEXT
10572: command does, so it stays 1 through the whole of DATAIN and past `PHASE_COMPLETED`. The CDB
10573: dispatch keyed on that flag plus ring occupancy alone. A guest write during DATAIN that
10574: brought occupancy to exactly 12 therefore re-entered the dispatch, **freed the live
10575: transfer**, and issued a SCSI command assembled from the guest's own returned DATA-IN
10576: payload. MEASURED on the committed build: `freed 1 live transfer(s)`.
10577: 
10578: THE FIX is option B, a phase guard: the dispatch additionally requires
10579: `atapi_phase == PHASE_CMDOUT`. The flag conflates two roles -- *a packet command is active*
10580: (read at four register sites through completion, so it must stay set) and *I am collecting a
10581: CDB* (what the test actually wants). The phase distinguishes them; clearing the flag would
10582: have broken the completion handshake.
10583: 
