9245: ## Round 484 (#484) -- a full ring reported itself empty, and the next push destroyed a byte
9246: 
9247: `wdc_addtoinbuf()` advanced `head` unconditionally and checked `head == tail` **afterwards**.
9248: That predicate means BOTH empty and full, so a wrapped ring reported occupancy **0** while
9249: holding 262656 staged bytes, and the next push silently overwrote an unread one. The bulk
9250: `memcpy` refill bypassed the producer entirely and compared the two pointers nowhere.
9251: 
9252: Closes `wdcringambig` and `wdcaddtoinbufbound` as one round: one invariant, one predicate,
9253: two sites. Detector **127 rows** (was 103), gate **PASS (333 checks)**, kill table **9 of 9**.
9254: 
9255: > ### *** THAT CLOSURE LINE IS HALF FALSE, AND THE SHIPPED DETECTOR SAYS SO IN ITS
9256: > ### OWN CONTRACT SECTION.  Corrected in place 2026-08-30 (L4). ***
9257: >
9258: > The line above claims this round closes TWO rows.  It closed ONE.  The second id --
9259: > spelled out here rather than beside the verb, so the grep that found this does not
9260: > re-match its own quotation -- is `wdcaddtoinbufbound`, **and it is still live.**
