7959: ## Round 494 (#494) -- the alt-ctrl handler never decoded its own offset, so a store to a read-only register aborted a live transfer
7960: 
7961: `DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc_altstatus)` read `relative_addr` **zero times** across its 91 lines while
7962: `dev_wdc_init` registered the region **two bytes wide**.  Offset 0 is Device Control (`0x3f6`);
7963: offset 1 is the Drive Address register (`0x3f7`).  The handler could not tell them apart.
7964: 
7965: `#481` corrected the reset bit from `WDCTL_4BIT` to `WDCTL_RST` -- right for offset 0 -- and
7966: wired in `wdc_new_command_reset()`.  From then on a guest store to a register it may not write
7967: **aborted a live ATA write and purged the queued ring**.
7968: 
7969: Measured at rung 3 on unmodified `evbmips -e malta`, reached through the machine's own
7970: committed `piix4_ide` path with no `device_add`, free-running.  Both legs from
7971: `git archive HEAD` (the fix leg patched with the round's own working diff), built in `/tmp`;
7972: **no project build tree was touched and no `.MUTANT` sentinel is owed.**  The two-part
7973: clearance was RUN per leg rather than asserted.
7974: 
7975:     P1  accept    0x3f6 SRST aborts the write        50 on both legs   unchanged
7737: ## Round 495 (#495) -- a host store during an ATAPI DATA-IN transfer was pushed into the ring, and the guest read its own bytes back as disk data
7738: 
7739: Real ATAPI hardware never accepts host data-register stores while the direction is
7740: device-to-host.  This model accepted them.  The injected bytes grew `inbuf_head`
7741: **without growing `atapi_len`** -- which counts only what the DATA-IN arms pushed -- so
7742: they sat ahead of the next chunk, the re-arm refilled on top of them, and the guest's
7743: whole next chunk arrived shifted.
7744: 
7745: **AND IT WAS SILENT.**  The one diagnostic that could have announced a stray write
7746: requires `!atapi_cmd_in_progress`, and that flag is 1 for the whole of DATA-IN.  The
7747: single guard that might have caught this was disabled exactly when it was needed.
7748: 
7749: ### The witness -- rung 3, and it had to be asked for
7750: 
7751: Unmodified `evbmips -e malta`, wdc via the machine's own committed `bus_pci`/`piix4_ide`
7752: path with no `device_add`, free-running, 1.52 s.  **20 of 20 predictions across both
7753: legs** (`wdcaddtoinbufbound/killtable_20260830/RESULTS_shipped.txt`, sentinel `ADDTOINBUF_RUNG3_DONE`):
7435: ## Round wdcatapistswap -- the accept side of #495's guard, and a floor breach found
7436: ## while pinning it
7437: 
7438: **A HARNESS round: no product code changed.**  It adds two accept arms to the rung-3
7439: detector `#495` shipped, closing a measured hole in its accept side.  What arrived as
7440: *"one accept row"* left as **two vehicles, three access widths and a repaired floor**,
7441: every step of that growth forced by a seat.
7442: 
7443: ### The hole
7444: 
7445: Swap the guard's flag operand for `d->atapi_st != NULL` and it escapes every row.  It is
7446: not a contrived edit: `dev_wdc.c`'s own comment records that `atapi_cmd_in_progress`
7447: *conflates two roles* and files exactly this refactor.  `atapi_st` has ONE write site and
7448: **no clear site anywhere**, so after a single packet command the swapped guard refuses
7449: every ordinary data-port write for the life of the device.
7450: 
7451: ### *** THE BRIEF'S OWN MECHANISM WAS WRONG, AND A PACKET-FED SEAT REFUTED IT FROM THE
8571: ## Round 489 (#489) -- a guest-supplied length ended the host, and deleting the guard would have been worse than the kill
8572: 
8573: `handle_command()`'s `case 0x14` (Read Table-Of-Contents) read:
8574: 
8575: ```c
8576: 	if (d->cnt != 408) {
8577: 		fatal("GDROM Read TOC not 408 bytes?\n");
8578: 		exit(1);
8579: 	}
8580: 	alloc_data(d);
8581: ```
8582: 
8583: `d->cnt` is **GUEST-SUPPLIED** -- two 16-bit stores to `GDROM_CNTLO`/`GDROM_CNTHI` -- so an
8584: ordinary guest register access ended the host process.
8585: 
8586: *** AND THE GUARD IS LOAD-BEARING, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS A DECLINE AND NOT A DELETION. ***
8587: `alloc_data()` mallocs `d->cnt` bytes, but the TOC writer below uses HARDCODED indices --
8361: ## Round 490 (#490) -- two guest-reachable host kills in the GD-ROM command handler, and a detector that a capped ask-again then escaped three times
8362: 
8363: `handle_command()` ended the host process on two ordinary guest writes.  `case 0x30` killed on any data format outside {0x20, 0x24}; the `default:` arm killed on any unimplemented command byte.  Both are reached by a guest assembling a 12-byte packet through the data port -- no harness, no direct call.
8364: 
8365: Both are now DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, the house idiom (`N = 1` per device instance, `(once)` in the message text), then `break` to the function's common tail.
8366: 
8367: ### The two things that were NOT obvious, and both are measured
8368: 
8369: **TWO latch bits, not one.**  A one-bit form was built (`M3shared`) and it ESCAPED: a session declining at BOTH sites sees the second fall silent, because the first already latched.
8370: 
8371: **`break`, not `return`, and the tail is why.**  The common tail zeroes `d->cnt`, clears `COND_DATA_AVAIL` and fires the completion event.  `M_retA`/`M_retB` measure a `return` leaving `cnt` at 0x98 with no event -- **two channels, both sites** -- so an early return leaves a guest waiting for a completion that never comes.  *That was a claim in a comment until the table made it two rows at two sites.*
8372: 
8373: ### Witness -- RUNG 3, and the evidence was already on disk
8374: 
8375: `probe:regress/gdrom_decline_probe.py`.  All three ladder clauses: the pre-fix committed build shows `fmt surv`/`unk surv` at **exit1** against a want of exit0 and the SAME script goes green post-fix; both mandated access controls pass on that pre-fix run (`live` 0x12345678 through RAM, `devsig sh4` 0xda74 -- the SH4 IPRD reset value, which RAM would read as 0); unmodified in-tree machine.  **The round had been carrying the weaker `detector:`/`none` reading.**
