365: 	    reassert_interrupts() is called from ten sites -- the GENCTL write arm
366: 	    calls it in the SAME access -- so ONE legal guest `st.b' of 0x04 to
367: 	    GENCTL ended the host, with no driver and no interrupt involved
368: 	    (witness: regress/pcc2_c040_probe.py).
369: 
370: 	    DECLINING TO ASSERT, rather than returning, is what the MIEN test above
371: 	    already does for the other GENCTL interrupt-control bit.  A `return' AT
372: 	    THIS SITE would skip only the INTERRUPT_DEASSERT below -- the IPL
373: 	    recalculation and the cur_int_vec[] updates have already run by here.
374: 	    (It is a return at the OLD site, above the recalculation, that would skip
375: 	    all three; an earlier draft of this comment attached that reason to this
376: 	    placement, which is a wrong reason for a right position, and a wrong
377: 	    reason gets reused -- `assert = 0' -> `return' at THIS line is a
378: 	    one-token mutant every shipped row passes.)
379: 
380: 	    VMESBCP2 (the MVME187 board manual) settles the shape in two places.
381: 	    The General Control Register page ($FFF42002) puts C040 at bit 10 of the
382: 	    word view -- 0x04, matching this fork's header -- and says a set C040
383: 	    sends the request "via the EIPL* pins at the level that is programmed
384: 	    ... in the SCSI Interrupt Control Register"; the Interrupt Priority
385: 	    Level Register page adds that IPL2-IPL0 "always reflect the encoded
386: 	    request level".  So C040 selects the DELIVERY PIN and not the level,
387: 	    which is why the recalculation above must stay.  MIEN, by contrast,
388: 	    disables "both the EIPL* pins and the INT pin" -- so the two tests are
389: 	    siblings gating one output, not a nest.  */
390: 	if (d->pcctwo_reg[PCCTWO_GENCTL] & PCC2_C040) {
391: 		if (!(d->warned & (1u << PCC2_W_C040))) {
392: 			d->warned |= (1u << PCC2_W_C040);
393: 			fatal("[ pcc2: C040 (MC68040 EIPL) interrupt delivery not "
394: 			    "implemented; assertion suppressed  (once) ]\n");
395: 		}
396: 		assert = 0;
397: 	}
398: 
399: 	if (assert)
400: 		INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->cpu_irq);
401: 	else
402: 		INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->cpu_irq);
403: }
